WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.171 --> 00:00:03.004
(uplifting music)

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.590
- I'm astronaut Nicole Mann.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.060
- I am astronaut Frank Rubio.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.713
- I am NASA astronaut Jessica Meir.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.040
- So there's about a million
things going through my mind,

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.820
as I think about going to the moon.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.590
- You know that the
little kid inside of me,

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:30.700
just gets super excited.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.450
- For me, that has always
been this dream mission,

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:38.450
this driving exploration and
curiosity of that next step.

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:40.060
- You know, just the excitement,

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.560
the amount of effort and the teamwork

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.050
that goes into such an amazing expedition

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.620
and really what it means to humanity.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:47.860
So it's very exciting.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.610
- If I can be a part of those
missions in any capacity,

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.393
it will truly be a dream come true.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:58.010
- I think it is important that in 2024,

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.360
we're gonna see the first
woman walk on the moon

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.640
and the next man.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.310
And it is a huge team effort,

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.400
but what it does, it
just shows the diversity

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.210
that we have in NASA,
in the United States,

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:13.715
really in the world that the
barriers are broken down.

00:01:13.715 --> 00:01:16.130
- I think for all of us that are involved

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:17.400
on this Artemis team,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.610
we would all be absolutely thrilled

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.310
to be the first woman on the moon.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:23.670
And to know that even if it's not me,

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.640
that I probably know the first woman

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.180
that's going to set foot
on the moon is something

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.663
that is so fulfilling.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.680
- So it really wasn't until
a little bit later on in life

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.889
that I realized that I
wanted to be an astronaut.

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:39.160
You know, as a young woman,

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.540
I was interested in math and science,

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.020
but I didn't realize
maybe the possibilities

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:44.853
that were out there.

00:01:44.853 --> 00:01:47.150
- My mom says, I first
started saying I wanted

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:49.210
to be an astronaut when
I was five years old.

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:52.420
Everybody associated that with
me all through my childhood.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.970
- My dream was always to be a physician

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:56.170
and to be an army doctor

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.210
and then to be a special forces doctor.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:01:59.540
And so I was kinda living my dream

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.250
and I was in my dream job.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.420
And then a friend recommended

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.060
that I try out for the program.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.490
So it was really very late.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:08.730
- I think for me it was
really a combination of things

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:11.810
of having this fascination
for science and nature

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:13.055
and the world around me.

00:02:13.055 --> 00:02:14.853
- The fact that I can continue
to be a part of something

00:02:14.853 --> 00:02:16.200
that's bigger than myself.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.760
- So many things out there, you
should never limit yourself.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.533
The possibilities are absolutely endless.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:26.030
- I think he's pretty excited.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:26.863
I think, you know,

00:02:26.863 --> 00:02:29.560
he's a little worried
about me leaving home

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.200
for an extended period of time, I think.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.033
He's only eight,

00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:35.100
so I think as any eight
year old kid would be,

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.820
but I think he's also very excited

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:38.340
and I tell you it's different now.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.190
Sometimes we always sit outside

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:42.410
and we love to look at the
stars and look at the moon.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.490
But now I think both of us look at it

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:48.280
with a little different light in our eyes

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.680
and a little different twinkle,

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.470
knowing that, you know,
someday soon we're gonna be,

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:55.130
you know, back there on the moon.

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:59.450
And hopefully someday he'll
be able to watch mom fly by

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:00.500
and walk on the moon.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.280
- When we do hard things

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.194
and we challenge ourselves as humanity

00:03:08.194 --> 00:03:10.310
to take on big challenges,

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.280
it brings us together and it pushes us

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.300
to develop new technologies, new ideas.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.320
- I think exploring space is so important,

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.600
really for the benefit of all of humanity.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.200
- This is just an innate
characteristic in the human species

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.050
to explore.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.520
- Science and technology development,

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.410
to exploration of our species as a humans

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:35.190
and where we're going
and where we came from.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.670
- Understanding more
about our solar system,

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:39.920
about our universe, about the formation,

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:43.600
not only of other planetary
objects, like the moon and Mars,

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:44.540
but of our own.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.750
- And you also see the
benefits back on earth

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:48.910
from science development,

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:51.410
and also just inspiring
the next generation.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.157
- We end up changing our lives forever.

00:03:53.157 --> 00:03:57.040
And I think for the most part
for the betterment of mankind.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.380
- When you look back at
the planet from above,

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:03.380
you don't see any of those
man-made geopolitical boundaries

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:04.950
that we've imposed upon ourselves.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:06.870
And it's so easy to realize

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.580
how we truly are all in this together.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:10.423
We are all one.

